<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ceres</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ceres was the Roman goddess of agriculture, grain crops and fertility, closely identified with the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a>. Her temple on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aventine-hill">Aventine Hill</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, traditionally dedicated in 493 BCE, was a centre of the plebeians. Because her cult followed Greek rites, Rome recruited its priestesses from Greek cities of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> such as Velia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p></div>
